# The two ways into a tsumego that nothing exercised: an SGF whose PL names the
# side to *keep alive* rather than the side to move, and a file holding more
# than one problem. Both paths worked; both were invisible to the suite, which
# is how the promotion and stale-snapshot defects next door survived so long.
#
#   ./tests/run_scenarios.sh tests/scenarios/tsumego_collection.scn

# --- PL says one side, the first recorded move is the other's ---------------
# Per the SGF spec PL is "player to move first", but problem collections
# routinely use it for "the side to save". autoPlayTsumegoSetup() reads the
# contradiction — PL[W] with a B move ahead — and plays that move as setup, so
# the solver is handed the position PL meant rather than the one recorded.
load_tsumego tests/data/tsumego_pl.sgf
wait_idle
expect tsumego true
expect board_size 9
expect main_line_moves 2
# One move in, not at the root: the B move was consumed as setup.
expect view_position 1
expect color_to_move W
expect phase paused

# Note what is *not* asserted here: the "White to move" hint. applyTsumegoHint()
# only fires at view position 0, so an auto-played setup move leaves the solver
# with no statement of whose turn it is — in exactly the case where the SGF was
# ambiguous enough to need one. autoPlayTsumegoSetup()'s own comment says the
# hint is applied after the forward step, so the intent is there; the guard
# refuses it. Left as it is rather than pinned either way.

# W[bh] is the recorded answer: SGF row 'h' = 7, so board row 9-1-7 = 1.
click 1 1
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 1 1
wait_until msg tsumego_solved
wait_idle
expect view_position 2
expect at_end true

# --- A file of several problems ---------------------------------------------
# Both games are the same shape, the second mirrored into the opposite corner,
# so a switch that quietly kept the first board would still look plausible —
# only the player name and the solution's coordinates give it away.
load_tsumego tests/data/tsumego_two.sgf
wait_idle
expect tsumego true
expect game_index 0
expect black_player One
expect view_position 0
expect comment Black to move

# Stepping to the next problem keeps tsumego mode: switchGame() re-arms it and
# re-applies suppressSessionCopy, because loading is not what set them here.
next_game
wait_idle
expect tsumego true
expect game_index 1
expect view_position 0
expect main_line_moves 2
expect comment Black to move

# The name does *not* follow the switch: switchGame() calls finalizeGameLoad()
# with matchPlayers=false, so the players matched when the file was opened stay
# put and the label still reads the first problem's PB. Harmless where nobody
# is playing, and pinned here so that changing it is a decision rather than an
# accident — the board underneath did switch, which is what the mirrored
# solution below proves.
expect black_player One

# The second problem's solution is B[bb] — SGF row 'b' = 1, board row 9-1-1 = 7.
# Getting the mirror wrong lands on an empty point and marks a wrong move, so
# the verdict below is what distinguishes the two boards.
click 1 7
wait_idle
click 1 7
wait_until msg tsumego_solved
wait_idle
expect at_end true

# And back. A solved problem is not a game worth keeping, so nothing prompts.
prev_game
wait_idle
expect prompt_active false
expect tsumego true
expect game_index 0
expect black_player One
expect view_position 0

# Leaving tsumego mode still takes an explicit new game.
new_game 9
wait_idle
expect tsumego false
expect phase setup
